concepts.content:
In a static scene where only the camera moves in a straight line with velocity $\dot{\mathbf{T}} = \mathbf{V} = (V_X,V_Y,V_Z)^\mathsf{T}$, the motion of a point in space $\mathbf{P} = (X,Y,Z)^\mathsf{T}$ relative to the camera is $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = -\mathbf{V}$. This is equivalent to all scene points moving with the same velocity relative to the camera. The resulting 2D motion field is given by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This relationship can be rearranged to make the contribution of camera coordinates more explicit:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= -\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

