concepts.content:
Lateral camera motion occurs when a camera translates parallel to the camera plane, such as when looking through a side window of a moving car. In this case, the forward velocity $V_Z=0$. (see figure: Lateral camera motion parallel to the camera plane.)

The relationship between the velocity of a 3D point and its apparent velocity in the image plane is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
-\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
 V_X \\
V_Y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The motion field depends on the depth $Z$ at each location. Objects close to the camera appear to move faster than objects farther away, while very distant objects appear stationary. This parallax effect is used in stereo vision to recover depth. The 2D motion in the image plane is in the opposite direction to the camera motion.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field under lateral camera motion. Objects close to the camera will appear to be moving faster than objects farther away. Objects that are very far (like the cloud) will appear...)

