concepts.content:
For a camera moving forward along the camera axis ($V_X = V_Y = 0, V_Z \neq 0$), the motion field is described by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

(see figure: Camera moving forward, along the camera axis.)

The rate of expansion depends on the ratio $V_Z/Z$ (the inverse of the time to contact) and is independent of the focal length $f$. For a camera moving in an arbitrary direction ($V_X, V_Y, V_Z \neq 0$), the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x - x_{\infty}\\
y - y_{\infty} 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The observed motion is zero at the focus of expansion, $(x_{\infty}, y_{\infty})$. Points near this focus (the point of impact) appear stationary, while peripheral points move faster and away from the center, causing the image to expand over time.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field when the camera approaches a planar surface. The motion field indicates the rate of expansion of the image, and it is a function of the time to contact. In this example...)

