concepts.content:
For a camera undergoing general motion with translation $\mathbf{V}$ and angular velocity $\mathbf{W} = (W_X, W_Y, W_Z)$, the velocity of a 3D point relative to the camera is $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = - \mathbf{V} - \mathbf{W} \times \mathbf{P}_t$. Using Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll) and assuming small displacements over time $\Delta t$, the rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}_{\Delta t}$ can be approximated to derive the 2D motion field:

(see figure: Rotation expressed by Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll).)

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This expression relates the 2D motion field to camera velocity, rotation, intrinsic focal length $f$, and camera coordinates. It is valid for small displacements.

