concepts.content:
When a camera undergoes pure rotation ($V_X=V_Y=V_Z=0$), the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Because this field does not depend on depth $Z$, observing the motion field under pure rotation provides information about camera motion and parameters, but nothing about the 3D scene structure. For rotation around the optical axis ($W_X=W_Y=0$), the field points orthogonally to the vector from the origin and is independent of focal length $f$. (see figure: Motion field for a rotating camera around the optical axis, $W_X=W_Y=0$.)

For rotation around the $Y$-axis ($W_X=W_Z=0$), the focal length $f$ significantly affects the field's appearance. At large $f$, the motion appears constant and similar to lateral translation; at small $f$, it resembles a homography. (see figure: Motion flows corresponding to a rotation around the Y-axis. (left) f = 1/3. (middle) f = 1. (right) f = 3.)

