concepts.content:
If a static camera observes a static scene while the focal length $f$ changes over time, a motion field is produced despite the lack of physical motion. The temporal derivative of the perspective projection equation yields:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{\dot{f}}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This results in a scaling (zoom and crop) of the image. Because the motion field does not depend on the 3D scene structure $Z$, varying the focal length from a single viewpoint does not provide depth information. This demonstrates that a 2D motion field can exist even in the absence of scene or camera motion.

